    "C258"
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Mannitol, a small polar hexitol, has measured absolute oral bioavailability of 63% in healthy adult men and about 20-24% in colonoscopy patients at 50-150 g oral doses; L3 oral exposure records show dose-linear Cmax and AUC after oral mannitol, demonstrating that very polar small molecules can achieve F >= 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C04","C02","C06","C10","C11","C12","C15","C20","C26","C32","C75","C79","C81","C78","C101","C103","C95","C98","C106","C84","C82","C86","C80","C112","C104","C118"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Glucose and propylene glycol, other small polar molecules, show high oral bioavailability (glucose F 0.722-0.843 in humans and 70-100% OGTT estimates; propylene glycol very high/close to complete), and sorbitol shows F 0.36-0.38 in shunted patients, supporting F >= 20% for polar small molecules.",
      "card_ids": ["C16","C24","C25","C54","C22","C46","C47","C07","C28"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 solubility records show related small polar molecules (erythritol, propylene glycol, glucosamine, gluconolactone) are highly water-soluble, supporting favorable dissolution for ascorbic acid.",
      "card_ids": ["C147","C164","C122","C136"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 bidirectional transport records for mannitol show efflux ratios near unity and no directional transport, indicating that small polar polyhydroxy molecules are not subject to gut-wall efflux; no ascorbic acid-specific intestinal metabolism/efflux record is present.",
      "card_ids": ["C216","C219","C190","C175","C217","C178","C171","C174"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 metabolic-stability evidence shows D-allulose, a small polar sugar substitute, was not metabolized in cryopreserved human and rat hepatocytes, supporting low hepatic first-pass for small polar carbohydrate-like molecules; galactose is listed as a high hepatic extraction substance, but that reflects galactose-specific metabolism and is not transferable to ascorbic acid.",
      "card_ids": ["C258","C262"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Low-F records (gemcitabine ~10%, sorbitol zero in normal subjects, glucose low in ponies) are explained by first-pass metabolism/clearance or species-specific presystemic catabolism, not by poor absorption; ascorbic acid lacks the gemcitabine CDA liability and has active transport noted in the query prior.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C05","C13","C19","C21","C27","C29","C30","C34","C41","C48","C53","C68","C44","C57","C65","C58","C60"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "neutral_or_unclear",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C258","C262"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for ascorbic acid is present in the supplied cards; all support is transfer-based.",
    "No ascorbic acid-specific hepatic clearance or metabolic-stability record is present in L6; allulose and galactose are indirect analogs.",
    "Ascorbic acid absorption is dose-dependent via SVCT1/2; the F >= 20% threshold may not hold at very high gram doses, but typical oral doses are not specified.",
    "Sorbitol zero-F normal records show first-pass can dominate for polyols; relevance to ascorbic acid is uncertain without direct hepatic extraction data.",
    "Some context cards have canonical SMILES mismatching the named drugs (frusemide, pipemidic acid, diflunisal), reducing transferability.",
    "No formulation, dose, or species match for ascorbic acid is provided."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction high: L1/L2 gold-context records from structurally related small polar polyhydroxy compounds (mannitol, glucose, propylene glycol, sorbitol in shunted/cirrhotic patients) show oral bioavailability at or above 20%, and L3 oral exposure records for mannitol confirm measurable systemic exposure of very polar small molecules after oral dosing. L4 adds high aqueous solubility for related polar molecules and shows that mannitol's low Caco-2 permeability coexists with measured F 20-63%, so low passive permeability is not threshold-relevant negative for ascorbic acid, which has active transport noted in the query prior. L5 adds bidirectional transport records showing no gut-wall efflux for mannitol. L6 is mechanistically mixed: D-allulose was not metabolized in hepatocytes, supporting low hepatic first-pass for small polar carbohydrate-like molecules, while galactose high hepatic extraction is not transferable to ascorbic acid. Low-F records are explained by first-pass metabolism/clearance or species-specific presystemic catabolism not applicable to ascorbic acid. The physicochemical prior is uncertain and does not outweigh this empirical pattern."
}

